{
  "ResearchDossier": {
    "topic": "why your recorded voice sounds weird",
    "summary": "You hear your own voice through air and bone conduction; the head enhances low frequencies, so your voice sounds deeper to you. Recordings capture only the air-conducted part. In Hughes & Harrison (2013), 80 people rated voice samples that secretly included their own and rated their own voice as more attractive than other raters did.",
    "angle": "The recording is the real you; you just prefer it anonymously.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "id": "c1",
        "text": "When you speak you hear your voice through air conduction and through bone conduction: bone-conducted sound reaches the cochlea directly through the tissues of the head.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/why-does-my-voice-sound-different/",
            "title": "Hullar, Why does my voice sound different on a recording? Scientific American",
            "quote": "Bone-conducted sound reaches the cochlea directly through the tissues of the head."
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c2",
        "text": "The mechanical properties of the head enhance the deeper, lower-frequency vibrations of your voice.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/why-does-my-voice-sound-different/",
            "title": "Hullar, Why does my voice sound different on a recording? Scientific American",
            "quote": "the mechanical properties of your head enhance its deeper, lower-frequency vibrations"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c3",
        "text": "A recording captures only the air-conducted component, which you then hear in unfamiliar isolation.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/why-does-my-voice-sound-different/",
            "title": "Hullar, Why does my voice sound different on a recording? Scientific American",
            "quote": "you hear only the air-conducted component in unfamiliar isolation"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c4",
        "text": "In a 2013 study, 80 people rated voice recordings that secretly included their own; they rated their own voices as more attractive than other raters did, apparently without recognising them.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://doi.org/10.1068/p7526",
            "title": "Hughes & Harrison (2013) I like my voice better: self-enhancement bias in perceptions of voice attractiveness. Perception",
            "quote": "self-enhancement bias"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "medium"
      }
    ],
